
# The theme for a terminal with a dark background.  Copy it to make another (NAME.theme in this directory: `nch --theme NAME`).
# The styles here are those nch uses (`core/line.baseStyles`: a test checks that they are all here).
# One style per line: its name, then any of fg=, bg=, weight=bold|normal|dim, underline=yes|no, italic=yes|no,
# reverse=yes|no (foreground and background swapped).
# A colour is #rrggbb, terminal (the terminal's own), or one of the sixteen of the terminal's palette (red, bright-red ...).
# What a style leaves out it keeps from the style around it; what default leaves out is the terminal's own.
# A colour can be taken from a style above: NAME.fg and NAME.bg are its colours as drawn (select bg=code.fg).  And
# v(COLOUR, GAMMA) is a colour with its value (V of HSV, 0-1) raised to GAMMA (more than 1 darker, less than 1 lighter):
# alt-code fg=v(code.fg, 1.2), v(#a0a0c0, 2), v(code.bg, 2).  Only a #rrggbb colour has a value.

default fg=#a0a0a0              weight=bold    underline=no  italic=no
em      fg=#ffffff
hide    fg=v(default.fg, 2.5)
select  fg=#a0c0ff
bad     fg=#c03030
good    fg=#209020
warn    fg=#cc5500
code    fg=#a080b0
file    fg=#a080b0              weight=normal
tag     reverse=yes

# alt-X: every other row of a table (all but the selection's and the usages') is in the alt version of each style
# (alt-default for plain text).  One not given here is the style it stands for: alt-code is code.
alt-default fg=v(default.fg, 2)
alt-code    fg=v(code.fg, 3)
alt-select  fg=v(select.fg, 3)
# the stops of a shade (a value as a colour: the calendar's --shade), cold (0) to hot (1), with any between (default-0.5);
# fitness-0 ... fitness-1 (and fatigue, form, impulse) would give one shade stops of its own
default-0  bg=#1a2a5a
default-1  bg=#7a2a1a
